360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (There are 29,460 customers who bought more than one product. However, when association rule analysis was applied, no association rules were found. Although customers purchased multiple products, they did not tend to buy the same combinations of products often enough for clear patterns to appear.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (Round 3: Customer ID to category)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (In this analysis one rule was found. This higher-order association rule shows that customers who purchased Books, Home & Kitchen, Clothing, and Toys & Games together were more likely to also purchase Electronics. \(confidence = 0.33, lift = 1.01\). This means that 33% of such customers also bought Electronics, but the lift value close to 1 indicates that this relationship is very weak and close to random. No strong one-to-one category associations were found, and the additional rules generated represent rearrangements of the same weak multi-category pattern rather than distinct associations.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (In practical terms, these results suggest that customers tend to buy popular categories together simply because those categories are common, not because there is a strong or reliable relationship between them.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
